      "id": 8,
      "title": "Trigonometry",
      "renderer": "manim",
      "narration": "Trigonometry is the branch of mathematics that studies relationships between side lengths and angles of triangles. One of the most foundational concepts is the unit circle, a circle with a radius of exactly one unit centered at the origin of a Cartesian plane. For any point on this circle, we can draw a line from the origin to that point. The angle this line makes with the positive x-axis is called theta (θ). The magic of the unit circle is that the coordinates of this point are given directly by the trigonometric functions: the x-coordinate is $\\cos(\\theta)$ and the y-coordinate is $\\sin(\\theta)$. From this, we can derive the fundamental trigonometric identity. Since any point (x, y) on the unit circle must satisfy the circle equation $x^2 + y^2 = 1^2$, and we know $x = \\cos(\\theta)$ and $y = \\sin(\\theta)$, we can substitute these in to get $\\cos^2(\\theta) + \\sin^2(\\theta) = 1$. This identity is true for any angle and is crucial for simplifying complex trigonometric expressions. Trigonometry also provides formulas for combining angles, like the sum formulas. For example, $\\sin(A + B) = \\sin(A)\\cos(B) + \\cos(A)\\sin(B)$. These formulas are essential in fields like physics for analyzing wave interference and in engineering for handling rotations.",
